WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.600
After five years of negotiations, Germany and&nbsp;
Namibia have reached a compensation deal for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.840
genocide committed by German colonial troops&nbsp;
more than a century ago. But descendants of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.600
victims of the massacres reject&nbsp;
the one billion-euro agreement.&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.640
DW Africa went to the village in Namibia&nbsp;
where a German general gave the order for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.560
his men to kill tens of thousands of Africans. Our&nbsp;
correspondent Adrian Kriesch takes up the story.&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.040
This is an emotional place for many Herero.&nbsp;
Here in the village of Otjinene, they call this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:41.120
tree Ngauzepo. Hundreds of Herero prisoners&nbsp;
of war were hanged here by German troops.&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:54.960
"This would go around the neck, when they hanged&nbsp;
them. Sadness, certainly anger, yes, no doubt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.560
that a human being could actually be doing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:10.640
for what reason? For the reason that our&nbsp;
ancestors simply stood up and refused to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:18.160
give up what belong to them, namely, their land."
More than 100 years ago, German soldiers killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:26.160
almost 100,000 Herero and Nama in the former&nbsp;
colony of German Southwest Africa. In 1904 Lothar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:31.360
von Trotha gave the so-called extermination&nbsp;
order – it included women and children.&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.920
Now Germany wants to recognize this as&nbsp;
a genocide – and has pledged 1.1 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.600
euros for development projects. That's what&nbsp;
the governments negotiated – without Rukoro.&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.760
"This is a deal between two governments,&nbsp;
about development aid. It has nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:58.880
to do with our demand for reparations.&nbsp;
They are fooling us. We are not fools."&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:06.160
Rukoro, a former Attorney General in Namibia, and&nbsp;
his supporters are still considering legal action.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.720
But previous attempts have already&nbsp;
failed in several US courts.&nbsp;

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.480
"Paramount Chief Rukoro says&nbsp;
that he's representing 95 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.480
of the Hereros in the country, but not&nbsp;
everybody in Namibia sees it that way."&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.840
We are on our way to the village of&nbsp;
Okakarara, two hours away by car. Here too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.640
the majority of the population is Herero&nbsp;
and there are several traditional leaders.&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:38.480
"The Paramount Chief is not in existence
"So you don't see him as your paramount chief?"&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.640
"He is not, it’s not that I’m&nbsp;
not seeing it. He is not."&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.360
The traditional leaders here don’t share a&nbsp;
singular viewpoint. Some support Rukoro’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.920
legal actions, others are in favor of&nbsp;
the deal negotiated by the government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.720
Some others want a renegotiation –&nbsp;
demanding more money for this poor region.&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:03.840
"Unfortunately. The negotiation team did not come&nbsp;
back to us to tell us how the German government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:12.480
arrived at the figures they have, so I am not yet&nbsp;
of the opinion that the two governments should go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.320
ahead with the agreement until there is&nbsp;
consensus with the affected community."&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.520
In the capital Windhoek, Zed&nbsp;
Ngavirue led negotiations for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.800
the Namibian government for more than&nbsp;
five years. The diplomat is also Herero.&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.800
"We would have liked to have&nbsp;
had more, but after five years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:43.600
it has been demonstrated, this&nbsp;
is what seems to them affordable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.840
financially, politically."
Ngavirue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:54.800
has a list of 27 traditional chiefs of the Herero&nbsp;
and Nama who were involved in the negotiations.&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.120
"And it’s only three who've&nbsp;
said that they don't accept."&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:04.160
"So you say it's a minority basically?"
"Obviously. Now there are people of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:14.720
who are thinking of their own name, ego,&nbsp;
story, so I think one can't worry about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.200
one, or two or three chiefs&nbsp;
and their followers say."&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.600
The agreement is supposed to turn the page on&nbsp;
a painful chapter of the past. But Paramount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:41.840
Chief Rukoro and his supporters are against it.&nbsp;
They want reparations – and new negotiations.

